on/problem "The daily commute time for employees is normally distributed with an average of $40$ minutes and an unknown standard deviation. Tests show that $15\\%$ of commutes take more than $52$ minutes. Assuming a normal model, what percentage of commutes take less than $31.7$ minutes? **Round the answer to $1$ decimal place.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.75$ | $0.80$ | $0.85$ | $0.90$ | $0.95$ | $0.97$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ | $\\,0.2327\\,$ | $\\,0.2296\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ | $\\,0.2643\\,$ | $\\,0.2611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of commutes take less than $31.7$ minutes." "The daily commute time for employees is normally distributed with an average of $40$ minutes and an unknown standard deviation. Tests show that $15\\%$ of commutes take more than $52$ minutes. Assuming a normal model, what percentage of commutes take less than $31.7$ minutes? **Round the answer to $1$ decimal place.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.75$ | $0.80$ | $0.85$ | $0.90$ | $0.95$ | $0.97$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ | $\\,0.2327\\,$ | $\\,0.2296\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ | $\\,0.2643\\,$ | $\\,0.2611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$\\%$ of commutes take less than $31.7$ minutes."] [:db/cas 17592186587346 :question/problem "What was the exact rate of change of the inflation's value at month $1?$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ $%$ per month." "What was the exact rate of change of the inflation's value at month $1?$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ $\\%$ per month."] [:db/cas 17592186587346 :question/worked-solution "The function ${f}^{′}(x)$ models the rate of change of the value of the inflation, applicable in the domain of $f(x).$\n\nThus, the rate of change of the value of inflation at $x=1$ months is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in inflation was $1\\,%$ per month." "The function ${f}^{′}(x)$ models the rate of change of the value of the inflation, applicable in the domain of $f(x).$\n\nThus, the rate of change of the value of inflation at $x=1$ months is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in inflation was $1\\,\\%$ per month."] [:db/cas 17592186587354 :question/problem "![](images/52/5253ff44a3fb04cc09568e5289dd7e73d3e286651e257cf7efe20944ac57e439.png)\n\nThe function $f$ is part of the graph of the function $y=2\\sqrt{x}+1,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of months $x$ at which the inflation given by this function was $3\\,%.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $f$ is measured in $%.$*\n\n$x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/52/5253ff44a3fb04cc09568e5289dd7e73d3e286651e257cf7efe20944ac57e439.png)\n\nThe function $f$ is part of the graph of the function $y=2\\sqrt{x}+1,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of months $x$ at which the inflation given by this function was $3\\,\\%.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $f$ is measured in $\\%.$*\n\n$x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186588376 :answer/value "29%" "29\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588403 :answer/value "26%" "26\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588425 :answer/value "49%" "49\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588477 :answer/value "35%" "35\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588490 :answer/value "32%" "32\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588587 :answer/value "41%" "41\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588617 :answer/value "46%" "46\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588627 :answer/value "27%" "27\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588635 :answer/value "31%" "31\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588648 :answer/value "33%" "33\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588679 :answer/value "38%" "38\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588720 :answer/value "38%" "38\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588724 :answer/value "48%" "48\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588805 :answer/value "44%" "44\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186588810 :answer/value "44%" "44\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186591863 :answer/value "0.70%" "0.70\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186591865 :answer/value "0.72%" "0.72\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186591868 :answer/value "0.65%" "0.65\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186591942 :answer/value "0.62%" "0.62\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186592030 :answer/value "0.59%" "0.59\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186601674 :answer/value "32%" "32\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186601676 :answer/value "39%" "39\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186601731 :answer/value "42%" "42\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186601740 :answer/value "35%" "35\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186601755 :answer/value "28%" "28\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186605084 :question/problem "The net weight of flour in $2\\,kg$ bags is normally distributed with an average of $2.0\\,kg$ and an unknown standard deviation. Tests show that $5\\%$ of bags weigh more than $2.405\\,kg.$ Assuming a normal model, what percentage of bags weigh less than $1.76\\,kg?$ **Round the answer to $1$ decimal place.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where 