 square is shaded."] [:db/cas 17592186606849 :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f83619e7d87983f16471d204ee86d4d14e7820385d716a432ea96adcf17716c.png)\n\nThe percentage of the shaded part in the above shape is{{field-1}}\n$%.$" "![](images/7f/7f83619e7d87983f16471d204ee86d4d14e7820385d716a432ea96adcf17716c.png)\n\nThe percentage of the shaded part in the above shape is{{field-1}}\n$\\%.$"] [:db/cas 17592186606912 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4a5c485a3788999fbfd064871aee3a8358148e52f6b44aec32e2fd5d258fe4d.png)\n\nIf a large square represents one whole, the percentage illustrated in the model above is{{field-1}}\n$%.$" "![](images/c4/c4a5c485a3788999fbfd064871aee3a8358148e52f6b44aec32e2fd5d258fe4d.png)\n\nIf a large square represents one whole, the percentage illustrated in the model above is{{field-1}}\n$\\%.$"] [:db/cas 17592186609241 :answer/value "70%" "70\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186609260 :answer/value "60%" "60\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186609301 :answer/value "75%" "75\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186609315 :answer/value "55%" "55\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186609317 :answer/value "35%" "35\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186609341 :answer/value "40%" "40\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186609347 :answer/value "50%" "50\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186609535 :answer/value "60%" "60\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186609536 :answer/value "50%" "50\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186609642 :answer/value "45%" "45\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186613129 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=125\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{125}} \\\\ 5\\pm 1.052\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=125\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{125}} \\\\ 5\\pm 1.052\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186613158 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=15\\,\\text{min},\\,n=25,\\,\\sigma =6\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\pm 1.960\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 15\\pm 2.35\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=15\\,\\text{min},\\,n=25,\\,\\sigma =6\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\pm 1.960\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 15\\pm 2.35\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186617243 :answer/value "12%" "12\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617246 :answer/value "1%" "1\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617253 :answer/value "9%" "9\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617269 :answer/value "4%" "4\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617270 :answer/value "5%" "5\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617273 :answer/value "16%" "16\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617279 :answer/value "20%" "20\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617287 :answer/value "40%" "40\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617289 :answer/value "55%" "55\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617294 :answer/value "10%" "10\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617296 :answer/value "150%" "150\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617306 :answer/value "12%" "12\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617314 :answer/value "67%" "67\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617315 :answer/value "10%" "10\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617318 :answer/value "40%" "40\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617319 :answer/value "16%" "16\\%"] [:db/cas 17592186617323 :answer/value "10%" "10\\%"] [:db/cas